//
// A fn-ref call's bind copies like its named twin (loft#1245, OPEN).
//
// `formal/binding.md` (B-Copy): a PLAIN bind COPIES the source, so the bound variable is
// INDEPENDENT and mutating it does not reach the source.  No `&` is written here.  The
// NAMED call obeys it; the fn-ref spelling of the same call ALIASES the argument, so the
// write lands in the caller's own variable.  Both backends agree on the wrong answer,
// which is why no differential oracle sees it.
//
// The IR says it outright.  A direct call mints a caller-local return buffer and types the
// bind with no deps:
//
//     __ref_1(1):ref(P) = null;
//     r(1):ref(P) = n_nm(have(1), __ref_1(1));
//
// The fn-ref call is handed no buffer, so what comes back is the ARGUMENT's own `DbRef`
// and the bind records the borrow:
//
//     r(1):ref(P)["have"] = fn_ref[1](have(1));
//
// Cause and cure are one family with D-clo-12 / D-clo-13: the fn-ref call site owns
// nothing.  The decided cure is the caller-local heap return buffer — the symmetric twin
// of `Parser::push_fnref_text_buffers` — not another ownership classification, which is
// measured ground (`formal/closures.md` D-clo-13).
//
// NOT scored here, and it is the same root: the minted store of a `??` whose subject arm
// borrows the parameter is never adopted either, so a loop holds one store per call and
// both backends abort at `store table exhausted: 65535 stores live at once`.  That half
// moves the PEAK, not a value — `LOFT_ALLOC_REPORT=1` reads `peak = N + 3` against the
// named twin's flat 4 — and a loft assert cannot read it.  loft#1245 carries the matrix.
//
// ⚠ The obvious repair is measured and is a use-after-free: relaxing
// `Scopes::callref_owned_return`'s `returns_borrowed_view()` decline takes the leak half
// from N+3 to flat AND the borrow arm keeps passing ordinary runs — it faults only under
// `LOFT_POISON=1`.  An ordinary run is not a control for this fix.
//
// @falsified-at: none — an `@EXPECT_FAIL` on an OPEN defect has no fixed build to falsify
//   against: the cell fails on HEAD by construction, on both backends, and that IS the
//   record.  What stands in for a control is the NAMED twin asserted first, so a
//   regression there fails the file for a different reason and says so in the message.
//   When loft#1245 lands, the harness prints `FIXED <path>`, this annotation comes off,
//   and that commit is the ref to run `make falsify` against.
// @EXPECT_FAIL: fnref-bind-aliases
struct P {
  n: integer,
}

fn nm(q: P) -> P { q }

fn main() {
  // Control FIRST: the named twin copies.  If this one ever fails, the file is failing
  // for a different reason than the tag names.
  b: P = P { n: 7 };
  r2 = nm(b);
  r2.n = 99;
  assert(b.n == 7, "control: a named call's bind must copy, source read {b.n}");

  // The cell.  A lambda that merely returns its parameter — no nullable, no `??`, no
  // capture — is enough.
  a: P = P { n: 7 };
  g = fn(q: P) -> P { q };
  r = g(a);
  r.n = 99;
  assert(a.n == 7, "fnref-bind-aliases: a fn-ref call's bind must copy, source read {a.n}");
}
